\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Trace in declared bases}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Choose bases #{(m_i)_{i\in I}} of #{M} and
#{(n_j)_{j\in J}} of #{N}, with coefficient isomorphisms
#{C_M:M\to k^I} and #{C_N:N\to k^J}. If
#{A=C_MTC_M^{-1}}, its coefficient #{A_{ii}} is
#{\ell_i(Tm_i)}, so the [intrinsic trace](fdip-003G) is
#{\sum_i A_{ii}}. For the arrays
#{F=C_NfC_M^{-1}} and #{G=C_MgC_N^{-1}},
\ref{fdip-00KF} reads
##{\operatorname{tr}(GF)=\operatorname{tr}(FG),\qquad
\operatorname{tr}(cA)=c\operatorname{tr}(A).}
The two products may have different sizes. Mathlib's
\leanref{Matrix.trace_mul_comm} and \leanref{Matrix.trace_smul}
express these coordinate identities. A trace pairing is not an
assumption; no totalized trace on arbitrary modules or extension to
finite projective modules is asserted here.}

\p{This is the basis realization of the finite-free calculation
associated with \citet{Exercise 22.6}{stacks2017stacks},
[Tag 02DU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02DU). The
[trace ratio](fdip-003H) additionally requires a unit denominator.}
